# What the image build does NOT need (deploy/Dockerfile, issue #19).
#
# The Dockerfile copies three things — environment.yml, pyproject.toml and src/ — but the whole
# repository is sent to the daemon as the build context regardless of what is then COPYed. Without
# this file that is 71 MB to move ~2 MB of source, and it is re-sent on every build. On the Pi that
# is the difference between a context transfer nobody notices and one they do.
#
# The correctness half matters more than the speed half: the context is hashed, so a `data/raw/`
# refresh or a mypy run would invalidate the build cache and re-solve conda for reasons that have
# nothing to do with the image.
#
# Everything here is either regenerable, machine-local, or already inside the container by way of
# the bind mount at run time (deploy/docker-compose.yml). Nothing here is evidence.

# The corpus. Fetched onto the Pi once and bind-mounted; never baked into an image, where it would
# be a stale copy of something that grows weekly.
data/

# Everything the pipeline publishes, including outputs/live/ — which IS evidence, and is exactly
# why it belongs in the mounted checkout rather than in a layer.
outputs/

# Tool caches. 31 MB of mypy alone, and the reason a `mypy` run would otherwise bust the cache.
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# History. The loop needs a repository at run time and gets one from the bind mount; a copy inside
# the image would be a second, divergent one.
.git/
.gitignore

# Tests, docs and the deployment files themselves. The image is the environment, not the project —
# the code it runs arrives on the mount. deploy/ is excluded via the exception below so that the
# Dockerfile is still readable by the daemon when it is named with -f.
tests/
docs/
*.md

# Local environments and editor state.
.venv/
venv/
.conda/
.vscode/
.idea/

# Secrets, in case one is ever written here by hand. .env is gitignored too; this is the belt to
# that brace, because a file that never reaches the daemon cannot end up in a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
